[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router0
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: the routing subsystem (FRRouting/route advertisement) on router0, which is the Location A gateway for VLAN 10 (172.16.10.0/24) and VLAN 20 (172.16.20.0/24).

Mechanism: End-hosts in Location A reach their SVIs (eth0.10/eth0.20) normally, but when they send traffic to external subnets, router0 either has no route and immediately returns ICMP “Destination Net Unreachable,” or forwards only the two hardcoded kernel routes, for which return traffic fails due to missing reverse routes. This presents to users as “nothing outside Location A is reachable,” while intra-VLAN and gateway reachability remain fine.

Localization: On router0, FRRouting is down (systemctl shows frr failed; vtysh has no running daemons: “ospfd is not running,” “bgpd is not running,” and running-config is empty). Router0’s routing table contains only connected networks plus two kernel static routes (172.18.50.0/24, 172.18.51.0/24 via 10.16.1.2) and lacks routes to networks such as 192.168.200.0/24. A ping from pc4 to 192.168.200.11 elicits “From 172.16.10.1 Destination Net Unreachable,” proving the gateway is the one rejecting due to no route. The uplink itself is healthy (router0 can ping 10.16.1.2).

Root cause: FRR crashed/stopped on router0, so Location A prefixes aren’t advertised and router0 isn’t learning external routes.

Corrective fix: Restart FRR on router0 and restore its routing config (e.g., enable RIP/OSPF on eth2 and advertise 172.16.10.0/24 and 172.16.20.0/24). After adjacency forms, verify routes to remote sites appear and that core routers learn Location A networks. As a temporary mitigation, add a default route via 10.16.1.2 until FRR is restored. 
=== END ===